<https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/408457> a <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/vocab#Place>;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "BAtlas 41 D5",
        "Bronson 1970 204-205",
        "Giuffrè 2026 ";
    dcterms:contributor <http://viaf.org/viaf/59162760>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/author/jbecker>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/author/sgillies>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/author/thomase>;
    dcterms:coverage "Igilium Ins.";
    dcterms:creator <http://viaf.org/viaf/17244949>;
    dcterms:description "A massive Roman maritime villa that stretched across multiple platforms and the hill known as Poggio del Castellare at Giglio Porto. It was continuously occupied from the first century BCE through the fourth century CE and was renovated and expanded multiple times. During the empire, it was equipped with a large fishpond (the so-called Bagno del Saraceno/Cala del Saracino), which had been converted from an original landing, as well as a lighthouse and possibly thermal baths. Subsequent structures have been built with and over the villa and some of its components are now underwater as well.";
    dcterms:modified "2026-05-12T20:07:17-04:00";
    dcterms:title "Villa del Saraceno (Giglio)";
    cito:citesForInformation <http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/43970336>,
        <https://engramma.it/eOS/index.php?id_articolo=6170>,
        <https://www.zotero.org/groups/pleiades/items/7C6UJ4Q5>;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/403221>;
    owl:sameAs <http://pleiades.stoa.org/places/408457>;
    skos:inScheme <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places>;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom [ rdfs:label "Barrington Atlas: BAtlas 41 D5" ];
    pleiades:hasFeatureType <https://pleiades.stoa.org/vocabularies/bath>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/vocabularies/fishpond>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/vocabularies/lighthouse>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/vocabularies/villa>;
    pleiades:hasLocation <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/location-of-roman-villa-in-localita-saraceno>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/osm-location-cala-del-saracino> .

<https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/408534259%23this> a <http://geovocab.org/spatial#Feature>;
    rdfs:label "Summanus, Aedes";
    spatial:C <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/423025%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/473375110%23this>;
    rdfs:comment "\"Aedes Summanus\" or \"Templum Summanus\" was a temple built near the Circus Maximus during the war with Pyrrhus. It could have been located on the slope of the Aventine Hill. The temple was likely built after lightning struck the terracotta statue of Summanus included in the pedimental sculpture of the Temple of Jupiter Optimus Maximus on the Capitoline Hill, causing the statue's head to be flung into the river Tiber.";
    foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/408534259> .

<https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/408534260%23this> a <http://geovocab.org/spatial#Feature>;
    rdfs:label "Mater Deum, Aedes";
    spatial:C <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/418516191%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/423025%23this>;
    rdfs:comment "A place included in the Regionary Catalogues for Regio XI, the Aedes Mater Deum is otherwise obscure. According to Richardson, the toponym may refer to the statue of Magna Mater on the spina of the Circus Maximus.";
    foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/408534260> .

<https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/409212%23this> a <http://geovocab.org/spatial#Feature>;
    rdfs:label "Ad Herculem";
    spatial:C <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/403253%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/403288%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/502042016%23this>;
    rdfs:comment "Now unlocated, Ad Herculem was a road station located between Vada Volaterrana and Pisae. According to the Antonine Itinerary, the station was located 18 Roman miles from Vada Volaterrana and 12 Roman miles from Pisae. According to Polledri (1967), this may mean that Ad Herculem corresponds to the area of Marmigliaio-Rimazzano-Crocino where three Roman milestones were found.";
    foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/409212> .

